  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Periods of rain tonight through Friday, along with \ngusty winds. \n\nLow stratus will persist this morning across most of the region with \na shallow layer of moisture trapped beneath the inversion in low \nlevel northeast flow. The stratus deck will quickly shrink in \ncoverage later this morning through early afternoon, with a brief \nperiod of sunshine before mid/high clouds increase ahead of the next \nsystem late today.\n\nStream phasing is underway across the mid-section of the nation, \nwith the resulting sharp mid level trough advancing east into the \nGreat Lakes tonight through Friday. Surface low pressure will \norganize over the Midwest today, then move to southern Ontario \nprovince by Friday morning before reaching the Saint Lawrence Valley \nby Friday afternoon. Model consensus has converged on a solution \ntracking the surface low just north of the area, allowing the warm \nsector to briefly build into the lower Great Lakes.\n\nA period of strong warm advection and moisture transport, enhanced \nby a 50-60 knot low level jet, will arrive overnight and support an \ninitial wave of rainfall. This rain will arrive in Western NY by \naround midnight, then rapidly spread northeast overnight. Additional \nperiods of rain will continue Friday through Friday evening as the \ntrailing cold front moves southeast across the region, supported by \nmid level height falls and right entrance region jet dynamics. \nMarginal instability in the warm sector may support a few embedded \nthunderstorms as well late tonight through Friday.\n\nRainfall amounts will vary widely across the area, generally ranging \nfrom 0.25\" to 1.0\". Terrain will play an important role given the \nstrong boundary layer flow, with downslope areas of the Lake Erie \nshore and Genesee Valley likely on the low end of the range, while \nhigher terrain is more likely to be on the higher end of the range \nfrom upslope enhancement. There may be a few small areas that exceed \n1.0\" if embedded convection develops.\n\nIt will become quite windy late tonight through Friday. A 50-60 knot \nlow level jet will propagate across the eastern Great Lakes late \ntonight through Friday morning. Gusts will increase into the 25-35 \nmph range areawide, with gusts possibly reaching or exceeding 40 mph \nin downslope areas along the Lake Erie shore and north of the Tug \nHill Plateau briefly. Boundary layer flow will quickly swing around \nto the southwest Friday morning through mid afternoon, with gusts \nstill in the 25-35 mph range in most areas, and up to 40 mph \nnortheast of Lake Erie across the Niagara Frontier.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Much colder Friday night through Saturday night with \na few wet snowflakes possible early Saturday morning.\n\nStrong cold advection will develop Friday night in the wake of the \ndeparting low, with 850MB temps dropping into the -5C to -8C range \nby Saturday morning. The cold air aloft will support increasing lake \ninduced instability overnight with a brief lake response, but short \nNNW fetch and a rapidly drying synoptic scale airmass will keep lake \neffect muted and brief. Lake effect and upslope rain showers will \ndevelop southeast of the lakes Friday night, with a few wet \nsnowflakes possibly mixing in very late Friday night through early \nSaturday morning before the lake effect showers quickly end by late \nmorning. The best chance of a few snowflakes will be across higher \nterrain, but no accumulation is expected.\n\nThe coldest airmass of the fall season so far will arrive for \nSaturday, with highs only in the 40s areawide, and even upper 30s \nacross the highest terrain despite some afternoon sunshine. \nTemperatures will drop back into the low to mid 30s Saturday night \non the lake plains, with some 20s likely across the Southern Tier \nvalleys and North Country. The cold air will quickly depart, with \ntemperatures back above average by early next week.",
